{"name":"Old Ruin","hex":"#917b53","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"old","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Mochi."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of an object, concept, relationship, etc., having existed for a relatively long period of time.","Of a living being, having lived for most of the expected years."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/old"},{"term":"ruin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The remains of a destroyed or dilapidated construction, such as a house or castle.","The state of being a ruin, destroyed or decayed."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause the fiscal ruin of; to bankrupt or drive out of business.","To destroy or render something no longer usable or operable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ruin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ruins","blocks":[{"text":"Ruins are the remains of a civilization's architecture. The term refers to formerly intact structures that have fallen into a state of partial or total disrepair over time due to a variety of factors, such as lack of maintenance, deliberate destruction by humans, or uncontrollable destruction by natural phenomena. The most common root causes that yield ruins in their wake are natural disasters, armed conflict, and population decline, with many structures becoming progressively derelict over time due to long-term weathering and scavenging."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Gymnasion_und_Therme_aus_S%C3%BCdwest.jpg/330px-Gymnasion_und_Therme_aus_S%C3%BCdwest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruins","wikidata":"Q109607"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Surb_Amenaprkitch.jpeg/500px-Surb_Amenaprkitch.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/